e_note_update` immediately returns 0 because `[[ \"$id\" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]` fails, so DSRACE-3's requirement (the loser PATCHes its note body to \"not started — concurrent claim: `<winner>` was first\") is silently not met. (plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh: \"_ccgl_race_note_post_v note_id \"$_CCGL_SLUG\" \"$issue\" \"$my_holder\" || note_rc=$?\"; happens when: GitHub's comment-create response arrives without an \"id\" field (malformed reply, rate-limit truncation, or a future API change) while two runs claim at once; the note exists on GitHub and participates in arbitration, but the loser leaves it unupdated, violating DSRACE-3.; fix: test; ACCEPTED IF: when the note was posted (rc 0 or rc 2 from _ccgl_race_note_post_v) but no numeric id is known and the run loses the race, DSRACE-3 is either satisfied by an alternative update path, or the design explicitly accepts the gap and the test for DSRACE-3 covers the rc-2 case.); the same kind of problem also at: plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh | _ccgl_race_note_delete \"$note_id\" \"$_CCGL_SLUG\" \"$issue\"":"TAFND-12","DSRACE-5 And: the test checks that stderr contains the winner URL and the string \"concurrent claim\" but does not check for any language saying the winner was first; a message that merely says \"concurrent claim — https://github.com/o/r/actions/runs/42\" would satisfy both assertions while never conveying that the named run was the first claimant\nDSRACE-5[code]: A coordinator response with race_lost: true causes zbuild_claim_acquire to refuse immediately without entering the stale-takeover path, printing a message that names the concurrent winner covers: RQTIE-1\n  Given: claim_coordinator_claim returns {\"acquired\": false, \"holder\": \"<winner>\", \"race_lost\": true}\n  When: zbuild_claim_acquire processes the response\n  Then: the stale-takeover hook is not called\n  And: stderr names the concurrent winner as the first claimant [FIX IN TEST: the test checks that stde